    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/sju/job/Philadelphia---Hawk-Hill/Senior-Administrative-Assistant--Academy-of-Food-Marketing_JR101296": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Assistant, Academy of Food Marketing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Senior Administrative Assistant, Academy of Food Marketing Time Type: Full time Position Summary and Qualifications: The Senior Administrative Assistant plays a key role in the daily operations of the Academy of Food Marketing (AFM) and the Food Marketing Educational Foundation (FMEF). This position is responsible for managing line-item budgets, ensuring accurate accounting and reporting, supporting scholarship administration, and serving as a liaison with vendors, university departments, and board members. The role also includes office management and executive-level administrative support and requires the ability to maintain confidentiality with student and financial records. This is a high-impact role that supports the mission of AFM and FMEF by ensuring operational excellence, financial integrity, and smooth coordination of initiatives, events, and communications. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Financial Operations & Budget Management Manage daily accounts payable and receivable, including invoice processing, revenue collection, and reconciliation of accounts through the University’s electronic record-keeping system. Maintain and update accounting ledgers; collaborate with the Controller’s Office to verify transactions and resolve discrepancies. Prepare and file financial records and support annual audits and reporting, as needed. Assist the Executive Director in developing and tracking the annual operating budgets for AFM and FMEF. Work directly with vendors, auditors, bankers, attorneys, and insurance agents to manage AFM finances and daily operations Scholarship Administration Manage external Food Marketing scholarships: maintain website content, process applications, schedule interviews, and coordinate recognition events. Serve as the primary point of contact for donors, partner organizations and student applicants. Communicate with the Office of Financial Aid to ensure proper allocation of scholarship funds. Coordinate and serve as the main contact for department events such as the annual Food Marketing Scholarship Donor Brunch, including logistics, scheduling, invitations, menu, etc. Administrative & Operational Support Provide day-to-day administrative support to the Executive Director and AFM team, including scheduling, calendar management, and office operations. Serve as the receptionist/first point of contact for students and other visitors to the AFM suite Manage hiring, scheduling, and payroll for student workers and graduate assistants. Coordinate logistics for faculty travel and expense reimbursements associated with the Peck Fellowship. Event & Board Coordination Plan and flawlessly execute logistics for three annual board meetings and other events, including meeting space, AV setup, catering, materials preparation, name tags, travel details and hotel reservations, and timely transcription of meeting minutes. Maintain board directories, monitor tenure, and track member commitments. Co-op and Student Development Support Assist the Director of Co-op with co-op interview logistics, including booking interview rooms, creating and managing interview schedules between company representatives and students. Support logistics coordination for student experiential learning trips and events, including room and travel reservations and itinerary details. Communications & Project Management Help manage AFM communications: email blasts, event updates, website content, and promotional materials. Create presentations and generate customized reports for recruitment, student development, the co-op program, and donor relations. Collaborate on special projects and cross-functional initiatives as needed. Minimum Qualifications (Education/Training and Experience): Required 5+ years of experience in operations or administrative support. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail with the ability to manage multiple priorities with on-time deliverables. Excellent written and verbal communication skills; strong interpersonal skills and a collaborative mindset. Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite (especially Excel and PowerPoint), Google Workspace, and Zoom. Experience with web content management and basic social media platforms. Self-starter with a proactive, team-oriented approach. Preferred: Experience in a higher education, nonprofit, customer service, or retail setting. Physical Requirements and/or Unusual Work Hours: Standard office hours with occasional early mornings or evenings for events or meetings. Pay Transparancy: The estimated pay range for this position is $23.00-$26.00 per hour. Offered compensation will be commensurate with work experience and educational background. Click  here  to learn more about the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
    },
    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/sju/job/Hawk-Hill-Lower-Merion-Side/Adjunct--Graphic-Design_JR101322": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Graphic Design",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: Adjunct, Graphic Design Time Type: Part time Position Summary and Qualifications: Saint Joseph’s University’s Department of Art & Art History has a need for highly qualified adjunct faculty to teach Graphic Design courses on a semester-by-semester basis. Likely assignments include introductory and intermediate graphic design courses, depending on departmental needs. Part-time adjunct faculty will teach assigned graphic design courses, support student learning, and foster an inclusive, studio-focused classroom environment. Course assignments may vary each semester and encompass a range of design topics, with the expectation that instructors bring contemporary professional insight and pedagogical clarity to the classroom. Saint Joseph’s University (SJU) offers a wide variety of bachelor's, master's, doctoral, associate, and certificate programs. With a commitment to personalized attention through a low faculty to student ratio, SJU may call on the expertise of adjunct instructors to complement the work of full-time faculty across all of its colleges and schools. SJU appoints part-time, non-tenure track adjunct instructors on a semester-to-semester basis with no guarantee of consecutive appointments. Re-appointment is based on university need, student enrollment, and satisfactory performance. Applicants for these positions are screened on an as-needed basis throughout the academic year. Teaching responsibilities are assigned by the department chair. Compensation for adjunct instructors is commensurate with educational credentials, professional, experience and instructional background. Adjunct instructors are bound to the terms and conditions of employment at Saint Joseph’s University, including but not limited to the Faculty Handbook. More information about SJU’s academic programs is available online at: www.sju.edu/degree-programs. Please submit a Cover letter, CV with website, 1 sample syllabus and 3 references. Essential Duties & Responsibilities: Provide support and guidance to students Instruct an assigned course providing syllabi and other instructional materials Use of university learning management systems to deliver instruction Teaching assigned graphic design course(s) Preparing course materials, assignments, and assessments Providing guidance, feedback, and mentorship to students Holding regular office hours Reporting mid-semester and final grades electronically Using Canvas or another learning management system Maintaining consistent communication with students Secondary Duties & Responsibilities: Participating in occasional meetings with the Department Chair or program faculty Collaborating with course coordinators to ensure curricular consistency and quality Supporting departmental goals related to student learning and engagement Minimum Qualifications: Required MFA in Graphic Design or a closely related field OR equivalent professional experience. Professional experience in graphic design, visual communication, or related creative industries. Proficiency with industry-standard software, such as Adobe Creative Cloud. Preferred [ Prior college-level teaching experience Flexibility and responsiveness in adapting course content to meet the needs of students with varied backgrounds Strong communication and organizational skills Professional or teaching experience in UX, interaction design, or digital design. Physical and Other Requirements May require standing for long periods of time May require talking for long periods of time May require carrying course materials Modality: In-person only Pay Transparency: Adjunct compensation is determined by education and relevant work experience. Learn more about the adjunct pay structure and the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
    },
    "https://sju.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/sju/job/Philadelphia---Hawk-Hill/Program-Specialist--Inclusive-Excellence_JR101285": {
      "title": "Program Specialist, Inclusive Excellence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Program Specialist, Inclusive Excellence Time Type: Full time Position Summary and Qualifications: The Program Specialist supports the mission of the Office of Inclusive Excellence by overseeing and managing programming for the Center for Inclusion and Diversity (CID) and the Cura Collective (CC) across University locations, as well as maintaining the Office’s efforts to build a sense of belonging and maintain an inclusive campus environment. This position collaborates with students, faculty, staff, and alumni to create programs, events, activities, and learning opportunities in a team-based environment toward the goal of facilitating belonging, development, learning, and engagement. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Develop a comprehensive programming calendar that encourages community involvement and outreach for the University community. Provide programming and advising support to the student organizations for affinity populations that are funded by the Student Budget Allocated Committee (SBAC). Lead, plan, facilitate, and oversee student-centered events including the annual Center for Inclusion and Diversity Retreat, movie nights, heritage month activities, Taste of the World, Kwanzaa Celebration, Senior Celebrations, and others that may arise. Create and maintain marketing, outreach, and social media presence for the Center for Inclusion and Diversity; support and update the OIE website as needed. Process invoices, monitor budgets, and assist in tracking and reporting expenses. Collaborate with partners across University locations and represent the Office of Inclusive Excellence at Admissions Events, programming, training, Department presentations, panels, and other events as needed. Assist with outreach, professional mentoring, advocacy support, and resource referrals for students with underrepresented and under-resourced identities to increase persistence to graduation. Assist with events and activities to support diverse student learning, retention, and belonging, including the Running Start Program, Cura Collective programs, and our contributions to New Student Orientation. Serve as an active member of standing committees and ad hoc committees that address inclusive excellence, including Day of Dialogue. Support the hiring, training, and supervision of the CID and CC federal work study employees. Secondary Duties and Responsibilities: The person who holds this position has been classified as a Campus Security Authority under the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). Attend and contribute to OIEIE Team meetings and planning workshops. Liaise with the Tri-Alpha First Generation Honor Society advisor(s). Support work in other areas of the OIEIE. Partner and work closely with various university offices and departments. Support the assessment and evaluation of student participation and professional development. Represent the Office of Inclusive Excellence at some evening/weekend events. Serve on university-wide and student life committees as needed. Other duties as assigned by supervisor(s). Minimum Qualifications (Education/Training and Experience): Required Basic knowledge and conversant understanding of inclusive excellence and student development trends, topics, and initiatives in educational settings. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with a diverse student body. Excellent problem-solving skills, and ability to work independently as well as closely with team members to achieve goals. Effective verbal, written, interpersonal, and public speaking communication skills across settings and stakeholders, including diverse constituents both internally and externally. Strong organizational skills and ability to effectively evaluate details. Ability to multitask and manage multiple projects and deadlines. Proficient with Google Suite and web-based software (e.g., Drupal). Bachelor’s degree; or equivalent and relevant professional experience. Preferred General understanding of laws relating to equal opportunity, affirmative action, discrimination, and harassment relating to faculty, staff, and students. Demonstrated commitment to institutional effectiveness, inclusive excellence, and supporting the University Mission. Skills in planning, organizing, and managing funds, including fiscally responsible spending, and analyzing trends. Knowledge of Banner and Cognos preferred. Physical Requirements &/or Unusual Work Hours: Ability and willingness to work occasional weekend and evening hours. Ability to move about campus. Pay Transparancy: The estimated pay range for this position is $50,000-$53,000. O ffered salary will be commensurate with work experience and educational background. Click  here  to learn more about the University's approach to pay and benefits transparency. Notice to Out of State Applicants:   Saint Joseph’s University may only consider applicants from Pennsylvania, New Jersey, New York (not including New York City zip codes), and Delaware (Delaware residents must regularly be present for over 50% of work time on an SJU campus).  If an employee must relocate to meet these conditions, financial assistance may be available but is not guaranteed. Saint Joseph’s University is a private, Catholic, Jesuit institution and we expect members of our community to be knowledgeable about – and to make a positive contribution to – our mission. Saint Joseph’s University is an equal opportunity employer that seeks to recruit, develop and retain a talented and diverse workforce. The University is committed to the diversity of its faculty and staff so that our students, our disciplines and our community as a whole can benefit from the multiple perspectives it offers. The University seeks qualified candidates who share our commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion. EOE Saint Joseph’s University prohibits discrimination on the basis of sex in its programs and activities, including admission and employment, in accordance with Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Title IX Coordinator is responsible for overseeing compliance with Title IX and other civil rights laws and regulations. To contact the Title IX Coordinator, e-mail titleix@sju.edu, visit Campion Student Center suite 243, or call 610-660-1145. To learn more about the University’s Title IX policies, the process for filing a report or formal complaint of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, or other form of sexual misconduct, and the University’s response to reports and/or formal complaints, please visit www.sju.edu/titleix . Inquiries may also be directed to the Federal agency responsible for enforcing Title IX, the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights . Applicant Information & Disclosures Please review this article of important information pertaining (but not limited) to: Background checks, the Form I-9, Equal Opportunity Employment (EOE), and Title IX. Pay Transparency & Benefits Overview Read more about the university's approach to pay and benefits transparency ."
